  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King shut down Cincinnati over six scoreless innings, and the Padres controlled this one from the fourth inning forward.",
    "what_this_means": "King's dominance set the tone early. Four hits, six strikeouts, zero runs across six frames. That's the kind of pitching line that wins ballgames, even when the offense stalls. The Padres broke through in the fourth with four runs and never trailed. They left nine runners on base and converted just four of their 16 RISP opportunities, which on another night costs them. It didn't today. Kyle Hart and Wandy Peralta combined for three scoreless innings after King exited, preserving a five-run lead that felt comfortable by the seventh inning, when the game was already decided. The bats contributed. 15 hits, multiple contributors. But this was pitching holding the thread. One shutout starter and a clean bullpen behind him. That's a formula that works when the margin is already wide enough.",
    "what_to_watch": "Cincinnati never got close. The Padres led from the moment the fourth inning ended. This is not a narrow escape or a late breakthrough. This is what happens when pitching doesn't break and the offense does just enough to matter. The consistency question. A season-long tension. Didn't resolve today. They left runners on in the first, critical spots in the fifth and ninth, missed chances that in a closer game would define the loss. But King made those misses survivable. That's the margin they're working with: a starter dominant enough to absorb offensive waste, a bullpen tight enough to protect it. Good teams have that. This team showed it tonight.",
